# DC-38
C('D-38', S, 'Design: Close Work Order window lists posting date and the cost rows',
  ['Sign in as an Admin (a user with every setting, See Financial Data included).', 'Use a desktop browser window 1440 pixels wide at 100% zoom.', 'Have Internal Work Orders turned on in Settings > Work Orders.', 'Use a location that is connected to QuickBooks Online, with every ShopView Accounts row mapped.', 'Have a customer flagged as Internal customer, with one asset, and its default internal category left at General Internal.', 'Have a new internal work order for that customer with every line Complete: one stock part with a cost, one posted special-order part and 2.0 h of labor, shop supplies $25.00, made for this test.', 'Open the attached pictures iwo-flow1-16-07-close-confirm.png, iwo-happy-17-17-close-confirmation.png at 100% zoom beside the screen.'],
  ['Open the internal work order.', 'Click Close Work Order.'],
  [['The work order opens.'], ['A window "Close work order [number]?" opens with a Posting date field set to today.', 'Rows in order: "Stock parts $[cost]" with "Posted to [account]", "Special orders and sublet $[amount]" with "Already posted to [account] at the vendor bill", "Labor 2.0 h" with "Hours only, nothing posted", "Shop supplies $25.00" with "Posted to [account], credited to [shop supplies account]".', 'Under the rows: "This posts the parts cost and shop supplies to your books and marks the work order Closed · Internal. You can reopen it later."', 'Buttons Cancel then "Close Work Order".']],
  ['S3-R2', 'S10-R3'],
  note="Words in square brackets in a message stand for the value they name, such as the work order's number or the customer's name exactly as it is saved; every other word of the message must match exactly.",
  stories=[3, 10])
